course: dictionary
lesson: 4111
title: Glaciers: rivers of ice
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A glacier is a huge, thick mass of ice that moves very slowly over land, like a frozen river. Context helps you check this: «A glacier is a large mass of ice that moves slowly downhill.» The words mass, ice and slowly all match the meaning.
explain: Glaciers form where more snow falls each year than melts. The snow piles up, year after year, and is pressed down into ice. «Much of Earth's fresh water is frozen in ice sheets, huge sheets of ice covering land, and glaciers, slow rivers of ice.» Glaciers sit beside ice sheets, so both are big ice on land.
explain: Look for clues in «A glacier is a giant bulldozer and file». A bulldozer pushes and a file scrapes, so a glacier wears away and reshapes the land as it moves. Swap your meaning in: "a slow, frozen river of ice that scrapes the land" still makes sense.
fact: «Glacier» is a noun: a glacier, glaciers.
fact: Glaciers carve deep valleys shaped like the letter U.
fact: A glacier can carry huge boulders far from where they broke off, then drop them when the ice melts.
fact: Many glaciers are shrinking as the world warms, which is why scientists talk about «glacier melt».
fact: Temperature usually falls about 6.5 degrees Celsius for every 1,000 metres you climb, so high mountains can hold glaciers even near the equator, as on Kilimanjaro.
exercise: «A glacier is a large mass of ice that moves slowly downhill.» What is a glacier? | *A huge, thick mass of ice that moves slowly over land | A hot, sunny valley | A small pond on a hill | A fast river of warm water
exercise: «A glacier can carry huge boulders far from where they broke off.» What does a glacier do with boulders? | Turns them into sand at once | Floats them out to sea | Keeps them in one spot forever | *Carries them far away from where they broke off
exercise: Glaciers form where more snow falls each year than melts. Which is needed for a glacier to form? | Snow never falls at all | *More snow falls each year than melts | The sun shines every day with no clouds
test: A «retreating glacier» is a glacier that is doing what? | Turning into a sea | *Getting smaller as it melts back | Moving uphill | Growing bigger each year
test: Why does a glacier leave heaps of rock when it melts? | *It drops the mixed rock and clay it carried | The rocks float away on the water | It pulls rock up into the sky | Rocks grow on the ice
exam: In «slow rivers of ice», what does slow tell you about glaciers? | They race downhill | *They move gently, not fast | They melt at once | They flow up mountains
